--- machine\common\securitydescriptorbuilder
canonical: Machine\Common\SecurityDescriptorBuilder

Shared suggestions for programs that construct security descriptors.

These settings describe claims and remember applied conditions. They do
not grant access or change the meaning of a security descriptor. Access
follows the key's registry security. The Permissions Editor opens existing
keys and does not create this shared root.

--- machine\common\securitydescriptorbuilder\knownclaims
canonical: Machine\Common\SecurityDescriptorBuilder\KnownClaims

Administrator-defined claim names and suggested values.

Each child key names one claim as `Source.Name`, such as
`User.Department` or `Resource.Classification`. Sources understood by the
editor are `User`, `Device`, `Resource`, and `Local`. The child can contain
Type, Values, and Description. Missing or unreadable keys supply no
suggestions; an unreadable child is skipped. Invalid value types are
ignored without changing stored data.

--- machine\common\securitydescriptorbuilder\knownclaims\<claim> type
canonical: Machine\Common\SecurityDescriptorBuilder\KnownClaims\<claim> Type
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent: no suggested type)
valid: TS, TI, TU, TB, TD, or TX
applies: restart (when another Permissions Editor dialog opens)

Suggested SDDL type of a claim.

TS means text, TI signed integer, TU unsigned integer, TB Boolean, TD
identity, and TX bytes. Leading and trailing whitespace is ignored. An
unknown type supplies no type suggestion.

--- machine\common\securitydescriptorbuilder\knownclaims\<claim> values
canonical: Machine\Common\SecurityDescriptorBuilder\KnownClaims\<claim> Values
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (absent: no suggested values)
valid: NUL-separated text suggestions
applies: restart (when another Permissions Editor dialog opens)

Ordered suggested values for a claim.

The editor displays the stored order. Empty strings are ignored. A
suggestion does not constrain which valid values a condition can use.

--- machine\common\securitydescriptorbuilder\knownclaims\<claim> description
canonical: Machine\Common\SecurityDescriptorBuilder\KnownClaims\<claim> Description
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent: no description)
valid: descriptive text
applies: restart (when another Permissions Editor dialog opens)

Explanation of a claim's purpose.

The description helps a person choose a claim; it does not affect access
checks. Invalid UTF-8 in suggestion text is displayed with replacement
characters, rather than rejected as a registry read error.

--- machine\common\securitydescriptorbuilder usedclaims
canonical: Machine\Common\SecurityDescriptorBuilder UsedClaims
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (absent: no learned suggestions)
valid: NUL-separated usage records with tab-separated fields
applies: restart (when another Permissions Editor dialog opens)

Claim and value usage counts learned from applied conditions.

Each string contains a claim name, a decimal unsigned 32-bit usage count,
and zero or more `value=count` fields, separated by tabs. For example,
`User.Department<TAB>2<TAB>Finance=2` records two uses of that claim and
value. Here `<TAB>` denotes a literal tab byte, not those five characters.

Records without a valid claim count are skipped. Invalid value counts are
skipped independently. The final equals sign separates a value from its
count. A repeated claim name replaces its earlier record. Suggestions are
ordered by descending usage, then by name for ties.

The editor learns from applied conditions, not unfinished edits. It writes
only when the existing key grants SET_VALUE. Missing or unreadable data
supplies no learned suggestions. Its own edits update the open dialog's
state; another dialog reads the saved value when it opens.
